{"name":"Gothic Revival Green","hex":"#a0a160","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gothic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Gothic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gothic"},{"term":"revival","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reviving, or the state of being revived.","Reanimation from a state of languor or depression; applied to health, a person's spirits, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/revival"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gothic Revival architecture","blocks":[{"text":"Gothic Revival is an architectural movement that after a gradual build-up beginning in the second half of the 17th century became a widespread movement in the first half of the 19th century, mostly in England. Increasingly serious and learned admirers sought to revive medieval Gothic architecture, intending to complement or even supersede the neoclassical styles prevalent at the time. Gothic Revival draws upon features of medieval examples, including decorative patterns, finials, lancet windows, and hood moulds. By the middle of the 19th century, Gothic Revival had become the pre-eminent architectural style in the Western world, only to begin to fall out of fashion in the 1880s and early 1890s."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Oostende_Sint-Petrus-en-Pauluskerk_R02.jpg/330px-Oostende_Sint-Petrus-en-Pauluskerk_R02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gothic_Revival_architecture","wikidata":"Q186363"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg/500px-Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg/1280px-Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg/500px-Savannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Cathedral of St. John the Baptist, Savannah, Georgia, United States","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASavannah_cathedral_2015_17_046.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Tom_Tower%2C_Christ_Church_2004-01-21.jpg/330px-Tom_Tower%2C_Christ_Church_2004-01-21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Tom_Tower%2C_Christ_Church_2004-01-21.jpg/330px-Tom_Tower%2C_Christ_Church_2004-01-21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tom Tower, Oxford, by Sir Christopher Wren 1681–1682, to match the Tudor surroundings","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATom_Tower%2C_Christ_Church_2004-01-21.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg/500px-Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg/1280px-Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg/500px-Zdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pilgrimage Church of Saint John of Nepomuk by Johann Santini Aichel (around 1720), Czech Republic, historic Moravia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZdar_Zelena_hora_19.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Strawberry_Hill_House_4_%2829886640996%29.jpg/500px-Strawberry_Hill_House_4_%2829886640996%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Strawberry_Hill_House_4_%2829886640996%29.jpg/1280px-Strawberry_Hill_House_4_%2829886640996%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Strawb